Advorix Data Analytics Internship_ Excel Dashboard 
Summary Insights

The data selected for this particular analysis is the pizza sales data of a particular restaurant. The excel sheet labelled pizza sales data provides the base or raw data required for the analysis. 

The sheet has 9 columns with each column representing information such as pizza id, order id, quantity, order date, unit price, total price, pizza size, pizza category and pizza name. The number of orders and type of orders have all been put across the rows and in this particular analysis around 500 such orders have been considered. For the order date, the months of January, February, March and April have been taken into consideration.

The second sheet labelled table visualisation shows the same data in a table format enabling the use of slicers which is a crucial part of the excel dashboard. 

The third sheet labelled pizza sales working represents the mathematical working by the means of total and average of the quantity, unit price and total price. From this we understand that the total quantity of pizza sold across the four months mentioned earlier is 510 with the average being around 1.02 The total unit price charged is $8342.85 and the average unit price is around $16.6857 and the total of total price is $8513.85 and the average total price is around $17.0277 The total profit is $171 and the average profit is $0.342

The fourth sheet labelled quantity and revenue across months shows the pivot table and chart for the quantity sold and the revenue generated across the four months used for analysis- January, February, March and April. The most quantity is sold in the month of February at 165 with January next at 162 followed by March at 158 and April at 25. Even with revenue it is the same with most revenue being generated in February at $2731.9, January at $2713.85 coming second followed by March at $2662.4 and finally April at $405.7 This is a clear indication that revenue generated in directly proportional to quantity sold. Since February has the highest sales even the revenue generated is highest in February and April having the least sales has least revenue generated. 

The fifth sheet has the quantity and revenue analysis based on the pizza size sold. The four sizes of pizza sold are Small, Medium, Large and Extra Large. The highest quantity sold in terms of pizza size is for large at 221 pieces with Medium coming second at 144 followed by Small at 139 and Extra Large at 6. Even revenue follows the same pattern with Large pizza sales generating highest revenue at $4393.95 and at second having Medium with $2290.75 followed by Small having $1676.15 and Extra Large having $153 in revenue. This is also another clear indication that revenue generated is based on quantity sold. It’s also clear that Large size pizza is the most sold with highest quantity sold and revenue generated and Extra Large size pizza is the least sold with lowest quantity sold and revenue generated. 

In the sixth sheet we have quantity sold and revenue generated across pizza categories. The four pizza categories taken here are chicken, classic, supreme and veggie. Classic is the most sold pizza having a quantity sold of 159. At second place is supreme having 121 followed by chicken having 117 and finally by veggie having 113. Revenue generated as mentioned earlier is directly proportional or dependant on the quantity sold. Most revenue is generated by classic at $2349. At second comes supreme with $2127.4 This is followed by chicken at $2103.75 and veggie at $1933.7 This indicates that classic is the most popular pizza category with maximum quantity sold and most revenue generated by its sales. Veggie is the least popular pizza category with lowest quantity sold and least revenue generated by it. 

In the seventh sheet we have quantity sold and revenue generated across pizza names. There are about 31 pizza names indicating great variety. The revenue generated is once again directly proportional to the quantity sold. The most sold and revenue generating pizza is The Barbeque Chicken pizza having sold 29 pieces and generating $517.75 The least sold and revenue generating pizza is The Brie Carre pizza having sold just 1 piece and generating only $23.65

Sheets 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14 and 15 have column and pie charts representing quantity sold and revenue generated across month, pizza size, pizza category and pizza name respectively. Sheet 16 has a copy of this analysis. 

Analysis Conclusion 
Best Selling or Busiest Month- February
Least Selling or Least Busy Month- April 

Best Selling Pizza Size- Large 
Least Selling Pizza Size- Extra Large

Best Selling Pizza Category- Classic
Least Selling Pizza Category- Veggie 

Best Selling Pizza Name- Barbeque Chicken Pizza
Least Selling Pizza Name- Brie Carre Pizza 

Analysis Insights 
a)	The company must focus on increasing sales in the month of April. This can be done by observing consumer behaviour and retaining loyal customer base as well as attracting new customers through reasonable discounts and offers that don’t affect revenue and profits generated.
b)	The company can find new measures to make consumers buy the extra-large size pizza by creating a fusion of two or more pizza flavours in the extra-large pizza
c)	The veggie pizza isn’t very popular with customers so the company must find a method by which they can market and advertise veggie pizza in such a way that the customers feel like buying it 
d)	The Brie Carre pizza is the least sold. The company can improve this changing some ingredients and marketing of the pizza 
e)	All these changes must be implemented keeping in mind that the revenue generated and profits stay the same or improve and not decline. 
 

